WEBVTT

00:01:56.463 --> 00:02:00.607
in exchange for the power we let them hold
over our discourse?

00:02:01.022 --> 00:02:03.037
I think this question is so important,

00:02:03.061 --> 00:02:05.474
because even if today’s platforms go away,

00:02:05.498 --> 00:02:07.006
we need to answer this question

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:10.564
in order to be able to ensure
that the new platforms that come back

00:02:10.588 --> 00:02:11.738
are any better.

00:02:12.683 --> 00:02:15.644
So for the last year,
I've been working with Dr. Talia Stroud

00:02:15.668 --> 00:02:17.358
at the University of Texas, Austin.

00:02:17.382 --> 00:02:20.115
We've talked to sociologists
and political scientists

00:02:20.139 --> 00:02:21.355
and philosophers

00:02:21.379 --> 00:02:23.188
to try to answer this question.

00:02:23.641 --> 00:02:24.960
And at first we asked,

00:02:24.984 --> 00:02:29.346
"If you were Twitter or Facebook
and trying to rank content for democracy

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:31.748
rather than for ad clicks or engagement,